  "worked_solution": "We rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3>\\sqrt{3},$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 3=6,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the ellipse is centered at $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, statement II is false.",
